2465 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092465 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092465 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092465 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092465 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092465 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e1c96bbd-1fde-5a80-973f-5b7f6ca284d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093075 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093075 #edb/ref 1061 20088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093075 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a47c9656-c51a-58d2-a0dd-f88dacd21b14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093075 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093076 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093076 #edb/ref 1061 20089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093076 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96b57363-6414-5170-aa47-40a20b85159c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093076 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093077 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093077 #edb/ref 1061 20090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093077 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4346b6e9-b1f3-5e72-a0bc-acca6bf07e34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093077 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093078 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093078 #edb/ref 1061 20091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093078 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b551a89c-7046-5a16-b776-1bf76d285e27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093078 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093079 #edb/ref 1061 20092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093079 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80c21f2c-61fd-5d44-98f9-6a51e9812c73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093079 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093440 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the matrix ![](images/30/30880937eed54b00cb0ebe13bd2a46e2634e4193343b1db552b30836ed1e055b.svg) Given that $\\lambda=3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ how can we find an eigenvector that corresponds to this eigenvalue?\n\nRecall that a non-zero vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ with corresponding eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if \n\n$$\nA{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}={\\color{red}\\lambda}{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this equation, substituting ${\\color{red}{\\lambda = 3}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =3v \\\\ Av-3v & =0 \\\\ Av-3Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-3I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to $\\lambda = {\\color{red}3},$ we need to find a non-zero solution of the equation \n\n$$\n(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A-{\\color{red}3}I$ is already in row echelon form, and it has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. Moreover, from the first equation, we get $x_1=6x_2,$ which implies that the general solution is\n\n![](images/fa/fa0625bc99de3a27874b5695e72c9fd2e41ab618e49228086df7efcf7e1bb04f.svg)\n\nWe only require one eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}.$ Setting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the eigenvector\n\n![](images/bc/bcd87529387e7c400b7f5ccd896cfcbe3e42ab386a5b9d84b565f12752e2c4c3.svg) \n\n**Note:** If we set $x_2=2,$ we'll get a different eigenvector, namely\n![](images/83/83541fd42d19f0e3b17be1c36983d50d1382f36352168bce2d148126db7bf265.svg)\nNote that $\\mathbf w$ is also an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=3.$ In fact, we can choose any real $x_2 \\neq 0$ to generate an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda = 3.$ \n\n**Watch out!** In the case of $x_2=0,$ we get the zero vector, but this is not an eigenvector. Remember that eigenvectors must be non-zero vectors." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093440 #edb/ref 1065 3562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093440 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c585e113-f22f-5b5f-bfef-b3b81cd21acc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093440 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093441 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a matrix $A$ with an eigenvalue $\\lambda$, the **eigenspace** $V_\\lambda$ can be defined as the set of all possible eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda$ plus the vector $\\mathbf{0}{:}$\n![](images/78/78c2c84d57ef00f32675c6d56fa91af39e0680eadf05ec266b0bdab9a57a8388.svg)\n\nNote that the eigenspace consists of *all* (not only non-zero) solutions of the equation $(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, the eigenspace can also be viewed as the *null space* of the matrix $A-\\lambda I,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nV_{\\color{red}\\lambda} = \\textrm{Null}(A - {\\color{red}\\lambda} I).\n$$\n\nWe also have the following theorem:\n\n*Eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues are always linearly independent.*\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/e3/e3c63b7efcb927079757e6f0e3e008a6f5038e11957524f51e1b1f45fdecd5f7.svg)\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-2$ and $\\lambda_2=-1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are \n\n![](images/1c/1c5df8239dae6efdd15a27498a9535b855004e3570c8eff46534993b9ec1e75f.svg) \n\nHere, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are linearly independent (they are not collinear).\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can be* (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent. For example, the vectors\n\n![](images/c3/c303fb73d6fc039bfa536d6f7de3103e774d913d2514c10c17d59fcf48d5bf00.svg)\n\nare collinear eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ above that correspond to $\\lambda_2=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093441 #edb/ref 1065 3563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093441 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "98b63ece-dfc2-522f-bf9d-260df06b75a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093441 #edb/ref 1067 "Connection Between Eigenspaces and Null Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346312 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346312 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346312 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6dd4ff79-2cad-5577-9875-d43d33b96145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346351 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346351 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346351 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71558a79-a339-554b-a780-18bbfb559ed4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346733 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346733 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346733 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9e820d6b-c973-50c0-8a58-b0115a074639" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346948 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346948 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346948 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b71ae531-024b-57d8-8a6c-1de89592cab4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346963 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346963 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346963 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b98b0a14-d68f-5b3f-83e5-1f2d84851416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186346733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347706 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3c731f83-bf80-5ca1-a39a-fcc51046617d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348360 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348360 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348360 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348360 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348360 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ab0b5f3-c889-5093-b43d-5502efb2f927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348360 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =6.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354738 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354738 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354738 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "085635bd-dce1-5925-b4fe-de2210a5c78c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354969 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354969 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354969 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2482f9df-8528-5027-b294-ef43187efdb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355406 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355406 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355406 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "537c203c-ffd2-5502-a11b-30b204ae3ee2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355746 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355746 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355746 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7b1fcb5d-0201-5258-aa3e-803953e23aa4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355756 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355756 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355756 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c520c67-acfd-50ce-97ec-e3cbae30718e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186354738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186354969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186355746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356921 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "05c8c7c2-f9fb-500b-a17a-b3feb5a918f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357567 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357567 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357567 #edb/ref 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca1d168a-973f-5cf3-be95-af0c79523ae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357567 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393996 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393996 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393996 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393996 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393996 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "507cd94a-0757-5af2-9b87-e9063e728920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393996 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =10.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393997 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6dc5d1e1-a9ac-55f4-97d3-5950006030fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393998 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393998 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393998 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c57b3f88-81ed-58c4-9f8a-e3ad55ab73c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393999 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393999 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393999 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09c64718-21b5-5183-bfa9-c8d620351a27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394000 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394000 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394000 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11ba0d00-edd1-5efe-9ef6-92af724df99d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394001 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394001 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394001 #edb